<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ilansura</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.) · capital Ilansura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ilansura, a small kingdom of the Khabur region best known for the unhappy marriage of King Haya-Sumu to two daughters of Zimri-Lim of Mari.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ilansura was a city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ida-maras">Ida-Maras</a> district in north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> whose exact site has not been identified. It emerged as an independent kingdom after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> and the break-up of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> in the 1770s BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its king Haya-Sumu was a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, who gave him his daughters Shimatum and Kirum in marriage. Kirum's letters to her father, complaining of humiliation by her sister and pleading to come home, are among the most personal documents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> archive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a> c. 1760 BCE, Ilansura disappears from the record.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1800 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:23.88%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div>